SUMMARY:Milky Way · Caves Beach sea caves · Core rising through the sea c
 aves
LOCATION:Caves Beach\, south end sea caves\, below Caves Beach Road\, Caves
  Beach NSW 2281
GEO:-33.11041;151.64770
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=-33.11041,151.64770
DESCRIPTION:WHAT: The core rising over the Pacific\, framed by the sea cave
  openings at the south end of Caves Beach. The shoot window is set by the 
 low tide\, not just the sky.\nPriority: Optional\n\nWHEN (Sydney time)\nNi
 ght of Sat 03 Apr into Sun 04 Apr 2027.\nShoot window: 23:49 to 02:44 (cor
 e between 8° and 60° high\, sky fully dark\, moon down\, low tide).\nBes
 t composition: about 01:09\, core 30° high in the ESE.\nArrive by 23:04 t
 o set up and focus.\nSunset 18:47 · astronomical dusk 20:09 · astronomic
 al dawn 04:44 · sunrise 06:06\nMoon: new moon Wed 07 Apr 09:51. 12% lit a
 nd below the horizon during the window. Moonrise Sun 03:11 · Moonset Sat 
 16:39\n\nCORE POSITION\n00:00  altitude 17°\, azimuth 114° ESE\n00:30  a
 ltitude 22°\, azimuth 111° ESE\n01:00  altitude 28°\, azimuth 108° ESE
 \n01:30  altitude 34°\, azimuth 105° ESE\n02:00  altitude 40°\, azimuth
  102° ESE\n02:30  altitude 47°\, azimuth 99° E\n\nTIDES (BOM prediction
 \, Newcastle)\nNewcastle is the nearest BOM standard port. Treat times as 
 approximate for this site.\nThe window above is cut to low tide 01:15 (0.7
 2 m) plus or minus 90 minutes.\nLow water Sun 01:15  0.72 m\nHigh water Su
 n 06:27  1.67 m\nAt the best composition (01:09): about 0.72 m and falling
 .\nPredictions exclude swell\, wind setup and wave run up. Check BOM coast
 al waters and swell forecasts on the day.\nSource table: https://www.bom.g
 ov.au/ntc/IDO59001/IDO59001_2027_NSW_TP004.pdf\n\nCAMERA POSITION\nCaves B
 each\, south end sea caves\, below Caves Beach Road\, Caves Beach NSW 2281
 \nCamera pin: https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=-33.11041\,1
 51.64770\nFace ESE (azimuth 107°) at 01:09.\nStraight line from Neutral B
 ay: about 90 km.\n\nKIT AND STARTING SETTINGS (Sony A1)\nFE 12-24mm F2.8 G
 M at 12 to 14 mm for single frames. FE 35mm F1.4 GM II for a sharper multi
  row panorama.\n12 mm f/2.8: ISO 3200\, up to 19 s. 14 mm: up to 16 s. 24 
 mm: up to 9 s. 35 mm f/1.4: up to 5 s (NPF rule).\nForeground: separate lo
 nger frames at ISO 800 to 1600\, or blue hour frames from the same tripod 
 position.\nPeak Design travel tripod\, red head torch\, dew heater strap o
 n the lens.\n\nFIELD NOTES\n- Access: free parking above the Surf Life Sav
 ing Club. Walk down to the beach and turn right to the southern end. A tra
 ck over the rocks leads to the caves.\n- The camera pin is the beach. The 
 cave openings are not mapped\, so drop your own pin on the daylight scout 
 and check which opening faces the ESE core with PhotoPills Night AR.\n- Ro
 cks outside the caves are mossy and slippery. Some openings need crawling.
  Wear grippy shoes.\n- Backup night: Fri 2 Apr (low tide 00:30\, 0.80 m). 
 That is also the Bogey Hole night\, 30 minutes north\, so swap the two if 
 Friday looks clearer.\n- About 1 h 45 min north of Neutral Bay.\n- Dayligh
 t saving ends at 03:00 this night and clocks go back to 02:00. Every time 
 in this event is before the change\, in AEDT.\n- SEA CAVE SAFETY: scout th
 e cave\, the approach and your exit in daylight on an earlier visit.\n- On
 ly go in on a falling or low tide with small swell. Leave the cave by the 
 time shown\, whatever the shot.\n- Never go alone. Carry a phone in a dry 
 bag and a spare head torch. Watch every set of waves.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE YOU
  GO\nTPE Web (camera pin\, subject pin and time set): https://app.photoeph
 emeris.com/?ll=-33.11041\,151.64770&center=-33.11041\,151.64770&z=15&dt=20
 27-04-03T14%3A09%3A00.000Z\nPhotoPills: Planner > drop the pin at the came
 ra coordinates > set the date and time above > Milky Way layer. Use Night 
 AR on site to confirm the core path.\nCheck cloud (BOM and Windy) and ligh
 t pollution (lightpollutionmap.info) two days out.\n\nSOURCES\nMoon phases
 : US Naval Observatory 2027 table (aa.usno.navy.mil).\nSun\, moon and core
  positions: computed with Skyfield and JPL DE421 for the camera pin.\nCoor
 dinates: OpenStreetMap. Terrain horizons: SRTM 30 m via OpenTopoData.\nTid
 e predictions: Bureau of Meteorology 2027 tide tables\, Copyright Commonwe
 alth of Australia 2026. Based on Bureau of Meteorology information that ha
 s been modified. The Bureau does not necessarily support or endorse\, or h
 ave any connection with\, this product\, and gives no warranty about the i
