SUMMARY:Milky Way · Pittwater\, Palm Beach · Core over Pittwater and Ku-r
 ing-gai Chase
LOCATION:Pittwater Park foreshore\, Palm Beach NSW 2108
GEO:-33.59640;151.32002
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=-33.59640,151.32002
DESCRIPTION:WHAT: The core over the dark bushland of Ku-ring-gai Chase acro
 ss Pittwater\, with water in the foreground. Closest site to home\, but su
 burban glow sits low to the south west.\nPriority: Medium\n\nWHEN (Sydney 
 time)\nNight of Sat 25 Sep into Sun 26 Sep 2027.\nShoot window: 19:45 to 0
 0:05 (core between 8° and 60° high\, sky fully dark\, moon down).\nBest 
 composition: about 22:10\, core 30° high in the WSW.\nArrive by 19:00 to 
 set up and focus.\nSunset 17:52 · astronomical dusk 19:15 · astronomical
  dawn 04:16 · sunrise 05:39\nMoon: new moon Thu 30 Sep 12:36. 27% lit and
  below the horizon during the window. Moonrise Sun 02:55 · Moonset Sat 12
 :15\n\nCORE POSITION\n20:00  altitude 57°\, azimuth 267° W\n20:30  altit
 ude 51°\, azimuth 264° W\n21:00  altitude 44°\, azimuth 261° W\n21:30 
  altitude 38°\, azimuth 257° WSW\n22:00  altitude 32°\, azimuth 254° W
 SW\n22:30  altitude 26°\, azimuth 251° WSW\n23:00  altitude 20°\, azimu
 th 248° WSW\n23:30  altitude 15°\, azimuth 245° WSW\n00:00  altitude 9
 °\, azimuth 241° WSW\n\nTIDES (BOM prediction\, Sydney (Fort Denison))\n
 Fort Denison is the nearest BOM standard port. Treat times as approximate 
 for this site.\nHigh water Sat 15:49  1.66 m\nLow water Sat 22:45  0.41 m\
 nAt the best composition (22:10): about 0.43 m and falling.\nPredictions e
 xclude swell\, wind setup and wave run up. Check BOM coastal waters and sw
 ell forecasts on the day.\nSource table: https://www.bom.gov.au/ntc/IDO590
 01/IDO59001_2027_NSW_TP007.pdf\n\nCAMERA POSITION\nPittwater Park foreshor
 e\, Palm Beach NSW 2108\nCamera pin: https://www.google.com/maps/search/?a
 pi=1&query=-33.59640\,151.32002\nFace WSW (azimuth 253°) at 22:10.\nStrai
 ght line from Neutral Bay: about 28 km.\n\nKIT AND STARTING SETTINGS (Sony
  A1)\nFE 12-24mm F2.8 GM at 12 to 14 mm for single frames. FE 35mm F1.4 GM
  II for a sharper multi row panorama.\n12 mm f/2.8: ISO 3200\, up to 19 s.
  14 mm: up to 16 s. 24 mm: up to 9 s. 35 mm f/1.4: up to 5 s (NPF rule).\n
 Foreground: separate longer frames at ISO 800 to 1600\, or blue hour frame
 s from the same tripod position.\nPeak Design travel tripod\, red head tor
 ch\, dew heater strap on the lens.\n\nFIELD NOTES\n- Calm water gives refl
 ections. Moored boats make good foreground lights.\n- Ridge line across Pi
 ttwater sits about 2 degrees high.\n- Light pollution is moderate here. St
 op shooting once the core drops below about 12 degrees.\n- Stay on the pub
 lic foreshore. Keep off private jetties and moorings.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE YOU
  GO\nTPE Web (camera pin\, subject pin and time set): https://app.photoeph
 emeris.com/?ll=-33.59640\,151.32002&center=-33.59640\,151.32002&z=15&dt=20
 27-09-25T12%3A10%3A00.000Z\nPhotoPills: Planner > drop the pin at the came
 ra coordinates > set the date and time above > Milky Way layer. Use Night 
 AR on site to confirm the core path.\nCheck cloud (BOM and Windy) and ligh
 t pollution (lightpollutionmap.info) two days out.\n\nSOURCES\nMoon phases
 : US Naval Observatory 2027 table (aa.usno.navy.mil).\nSun\, moon and core
  positions: computed with Skyfield and JPL DE421 for the camera pin.\nCoor
 dinates: OpenStreetMap. Terrain horizons: SRTM 30 m via OpenTopoData.\nTid
